title: "Characterising the Gains to Autonomy in the Public Sector: Evidence from French Academia"
collection: publications
category: wp
abstract: ' This paper studies the effect of replacing central administration by local administrative autonomy on output, through the example of the 2007 reform of French universities. University management received in particular more power over their hirings and the opportunity, in consortia with other neighbouring institutions, to apply to large institution-level grants in 2011. The structural exclusion of Grandes Ecoles and national research institutions from treatment allows me to identify the effect of this change on researcher mobility and research output. Using bibliometric data from OpenAlex in a staggered differences-in-differences framework, I find that administrative autonomy increases the attractiveness of affected institutions, raising their number of researchers by 34% on average after the reform. Their total publications, citations and publications in top 5% journals increase by 34%, 19% and 33% respectively, but output per researcher falls: publications per researcher decrease by 14% and top-journal publications per researcher by 32%. At the individual level, researchers already present in treated institutions publish 7% less and 8% less in top journals, with no significant change in citations or novelty. Institution-level gains are therefore driven by size: autonomy raises total production through attractiveness, while output per researcher declines, partly because the researchers already present publish less.'

presentation: IFE-OFCE Seminar, Applied Economics Seminar, PSE; Collège de France and INSEAD Brown Bag seminar
